WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:03.803
Here's my mortgage balance in my bank account,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.606
sitting at over 144,000 Canadian dollars right now.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.108
Today, I'm going to do one thing,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.944
and that is go to the bank and pay it all off.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:11.811
Time to pay up.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:23.957
Damn, I wasn't recording, Shit! 

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.024
OK, it's paid off.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.193
And the feeling can be summed up in one word: awesome.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.061
But honestly,

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:30.230
this probably isn't the most rational investment decision.

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:30.730
Why?

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:34.267
Because making the choice to pay off my mortgage in one go,

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:35.802
I didn't hesitate for a single second.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.404
So should I have hesitated?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.106
You see, we often hear that NOT paying off your mortgage early

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:41.541
is actually the more rational move.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.644
The whole basis for this argument is the so-called opportunity cost.

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.513
Let's say your mortgage rate is 3%,

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.715
if you don't pay it off and instead put that money into the stock market,

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:49.616
like the S&amp;P 500 for example,

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.118
your long-term average return might be around

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:52.685
10% a year,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.687
and looking at the past five years, that number would be even higher.

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:57.223
And if you dumped that money into a crazy

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:57.991
soaring stock like NVIDIA,

00:00:58.057 --> 00:00:59.592
your returns would be absolutely mind-blowing.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.628
So choosing not to pay off the mortgage

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.963
and investing that money instead is the right way to go.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:05.665
And honestly, that's exactly what I used to do.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:09.502
Five years ago, when I had to renew my loan

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:09.836
contract with the bank,

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.738
the bank offered me an interest rate of 1.9%,

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.207
on a five-year fixed term. I said yes immediately,

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.377
chose not to pay off the mortgage early, and invested the money instead.

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:19.179
And as it turns out, that was the right call.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:20.113
Over the past five years,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.382
my investments easily beat my mortgage rate,

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.317
and later, because interest rates went up,

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:26.653
even just parking my cash at the bank or brokerage

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.755
earned me a higher interest rate than my mortgage.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:30.824
I literally had zero reason to pay off the mortgage.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.893
But why did I still choose to pay it off when renewing after five years?

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.297
Was it because the mortgage rate got higher than my investment returns and wasn't worth it anymore?

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.565
Strictly speaking, not exactly.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.934
Look, these are the renewal options the bank offered me.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.735
Just a quick heads-up: here in Canada,

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.138
basically everyone has to sign a new mortgage

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:46.306
contract with the bank every few years,

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.841
with rates determined by the current market,

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:49.409
instead of locking it in for 30 years right off the bat.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:52.278
As we can see, rates are definitely way higher now.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.981
The best deal was probably the one I picked: this five-year

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:56.749
variable rate option at 4%.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.118
But I knew I could snag some even better

00:01:59.185 --> 00:01:59.919
rates from other banks,

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.621
so while this 4% isn't exactly low,

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:03.823
if we compare it to the S&amp;P 500 we just

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.123
talked about,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.226
theoretically, investing is still the smarter move, right?

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.995
Not to mention, I have other investments that I consider to be

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:09.496
even better options.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:12.098
So that's why I say paying off the mortgage early wasn't

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.466
a rational decision for me.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.535
But why did I do it anyway?

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:18.371
Because I'm after absolute zero debt.

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.473
Ten years ago, when I first took out a loan to buy this house,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.875
I set a rule for myself:

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.244
the moment I can pay it off in one go,

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.646
I'm gonna do it and kickstart my debt-free life.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.982
Five years ago I couldn't, so I didn't,

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:29.949
but now, I can.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.885
So in today's video,

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:35.121
I want to talk to you guys about the issues hiding behind this rational idea

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.589
of not paying off your mortgage,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.057
why I'm chasing absolute zero debt,

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.326
share some advice on the methods to actually get there,

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:43.763
and explain what I mean by the word "can" that I just stressed.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.331
Hopefully, this gives you some inspiration.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:46.699
OK, before we officially dive in,

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.169
I want to take 30 seconds to thank my long-term

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:50.570
sponsor, Interactive Brokers.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:51.538
Now, IB

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.741
is an award-winning, widely recognized global brokerage,

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:56.509
and it's the platform I use to invest in US stocks.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:57.043
Why I love it:

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.045
number one, their trading costs are super low,

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.080
and number two, their currency conversion rates are highly competitive.

