By clicking the link, I do earn a commission,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.308
so it's a great way to support the channel.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:29.909
Alright, now let's get back to the video.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.211
First off, I want to clarify a concept:

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.179
exactly what does "zero debt" mean?

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:35.982
Zero debt means having literally zero debt.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.117
If your assets are greater than your liabilities, that's not zero debt.

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:40.086
Even if you have more cash on hand than your total debt,

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.421
that's still not zero debt,

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:43.189
at best, it's called being technically debt-free.

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:46.159
Now, I was technically debt-free three years ago,

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:48.561
but fundamentally, it's still not truly debt free.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.431
So why am I so hung up on this definition?

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:54.534
Imagine a seesaw: on the right side is your mortgage.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.669
This thing is fixed, it's certain.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.638
No matter if your income is high or low, if interest rates go up or down,

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.573
no matter what happens in the world, it sits right there.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.642
You have an obligation to pay it back, and it's not going away.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.812
But on the left side, it's a whole bunch of stuff: your investment returns,

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.313
your confidence, your mental resilience,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.682
your cash flow, your earning power, your health,

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.452
your family, global politics, currencies, and on and on.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.187
Have you noticed?

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:17.624
Almost everything on the left side is completely uncertain.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.593
Even if you put cash on the left side, it's still uncertain,

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.930
because that cash could end up getting spent on something else.

00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:26.933
So when you still have debt,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.803
you have to actively maintain all these uncertain factors on the left

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.505
just to keep this seesaw balanced.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:35.942
You're basically using uncertain things to balance out a completely

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:36.743
certain thing.

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.478
That's why deciding whether to pay off your mortgage early

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.947
is way more than just a simple math equation.

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:44.317
You're trying to sustain a system that could collapse at any moment.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.719
So why are you willing to maintain it?

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:48.855
A lot of people will tell you it's for the sake of being rational,

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.924
it's about putting your money to work in more efficient places,

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.426
all because of that opportunity cost.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.929
And I completely agree with those points, but behind all this rationality,

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.766
there are usually three massive issues that people constantly overlook.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.237
Number one: is your opportunity cost calculated by looking

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:04.837
in the rearview mirror?

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:07.707
A lot of people say, "If I hadn't paid off my mortgage back then,

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.343
and bought Apple, NVIDIA, or even just the S&amp;P 500,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.511
I'd be so rich by now."

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:12.445
But I have to ask you:

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:15.181
did you actually invest in those assets back then?

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:19.085
And right now, do you really have an investment target that you're absolutely

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:19.385
certain about?

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.321
More importantly, if you didn't pay off your mortgage,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.489
would you truly put all that money into investments?

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.158
Or would it just sit there waiting on the sidelines,

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.226
or end up getting blown on all sorts of random expenses?

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.997
Investment opportunities you don't know about are NOT your opportunity cost.

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:34.100
Investments you aren't sure about can hardly be counted as your opportunity cost.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.835
If you don't actually execute the investments,

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:38.504
the opportunity cost simply doesn't exist.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.807
But your mortgage? That definitely exists.

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:42.241
Number two,

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.911
a lot of us are actually looking at this whole thing

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:45.345
with a very linear mindset.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:48.314
We keep saying the S&amp;P 500's long-term annualized return is this and that,

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:51.417
or some stock 10x-ed in ten years, which is way better than paying off a mortgage.

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:53.019
But you're only looking at the finish line,

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:54.654
you're totally ignoring the journey to get there.

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:55.855
You know that meme floating around online?

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:59.125
It shows how you think your investment returns will look like this perfectly smooth

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.126
upward curve,

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.929
but your actual returns are basically a wild rollercoaster

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:03.796
full of massive drops along the way.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.598
If you want to survive in the long run,

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:07.166
the prerequisite is that you don't get wiped out in the short term.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.303
So I want to challenge you a bit and ask:

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.873
have you ever seriously considered the worst-case scenario? What if the

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.242
S&amp;P 500 goes through another Great Depression like in the

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:16.909
1930s,

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.311
followed by years of a brutal slump?

00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:21.147
Your investments are bleeding heavily, and then when it rains it pours:

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.683
you lose your job, you have health issues, your family needs your help.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.685
All the worst things happen at the exact same time,

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:28.387
yet your mortgage is still right there, demanding to be paid every single month.

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:29.722
Could you honestly handle that?

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.358
This isn't about you not having a strong enough mindset;

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:34.727
it's about whether you have a big enough margin of safety.

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:38.765
I feel like most people actually give themselves a free pass

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:39.632
when it comes to this question.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:41.601
Number three: the issue of complexity.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:44.203
Let me go back to that seesaw analogy. Maintaining all those

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.538
uncertain factors on the left side,

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:48.808
just maintaining the seesaw itself, is a form of complexity.

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:51.677
You're adding so many extra things into your life that need to be managed.

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:53.146
You have to keep an eye on your investments,

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.381
you have to evaluate all sorts of macro variables,

00:06:55.448 --> 00:06:56.649
you have to judge economic cycles,

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:58.651
you have to worry about whether interest rates will go up, and on and on.

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.854
In fact, just the fact that we're discussing this right now is proof of that complexity.

00:07:02.388 --> 00:07:02.989
Without the debt,

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:03.823
Without this seesaw,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.858
we wouldn't even need to have this conversation.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:09.562
I'm definitely not saying you *have* to pay off your mortgage early,

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:12.899
strive for zero debt, or that you're wrong if you don't.

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:13.900
Everyone's situation is different.

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:17.770
But if you decide *not* to actively eliminate your debt,

00:07:17.837 --> 00:07:20.840
it needs to be after you've seriously thought about these three levels

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:21.340
first,

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:22.642
to be a truly rational decision.

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:25.344
For me, I've considered all three of these questions.

00:07:25.578 --> 00:07:26.579
Over the past three years,

00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:29.115
my cash on hand has always been several times my mortgage balance at any given moment,

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:30.283
and my cash flow has always been positive.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:33.386
From a purely rational standpoint, I could totally just maintain the status quo,

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:35.288
let that seesaw keep rocking,

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.923
and keep looking for new investment opportunities.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:39.525
I absolutely didn't need to pay off my mortgage early.

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:44.363
However, not all financial decisions have to be perfectly rational

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:45.231
to make sense.

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:48.367
So earlier, when I was reading Morgan Housel's "The

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:49.836
Psychology of Money,"

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:51.637
there was one concept that really stuck with me.

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:55.842
He said that being rational and being reasonable are not the same thing.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:58.845
Like, a decision can be irrational,

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:00.513
but it can still be reasonable.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:01.848
Let me give you an example.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:04.784
Like a lot of people, I dollar-cost average into the S&amp;P 500.

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:07.653
My wife and I, our method is to set up automatic

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:08.154
trades,

00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:09.956
buying 1,000 CAD automatically every week.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.959
But statistically speaking, this actually isn't the most rational

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:13.426
approach.

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:17.230
According to the stats, you should invest all your money at once into the

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:17.830
S&amp;P 500,

00:08:17.897 --> 00:08:19.899
and your long-term returns will be higher than dollar-cost averaging.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.801
But even though that's the rational thing to do,

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:24.337
very few people can actually do it. I know I'd have a hard time doing it,

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:28.007
because I wouldn't be able to stop myself from holding the cash, trying to time the market, and waiting for the

00:08:28.074 --> 00:08:28.875
perfect time to buy.

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:30.877
And the most likely outcome is that I'd just keep waiting forever,

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:31.544
and never actually invest.

00:08:31.611 --> 00:08:34.547
So in the end, our strategy is to set our brokerage to automatically invest a

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:35.848
fixed amount every week.

00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:37.617
Then, when there's a massive dip,

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.886
I'll manually go in and buy a lot extra.

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:43.322
Now, that's not rational, but it's very reasonable for us,

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:46.525
because it ensures we keep buying consistently and won't regret it later.

00:08:47.093 --> 00:08:49.562
So Housel says in his book that as humans,

00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:52.532
in real life, we are neither completely rational,

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:53.966
nor completely irrational.

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:54.934
What matters is,

00:08:55.001 --> 00:08:57.737
if a decision can maximize the reduction of your future

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:58.704
regrets,

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:01.274
then it might not be the most rational, but it is reasonable.

00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:05.845
And striving for zero debt, to me, is exactly this kind of irrational

00:09:05.912 --> 00:09:07.113
but reasonable decision.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:09.849
So now let's talk about why it's reasonable.

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:12.752
Why am I striving for zero debt?

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:16.322
For me, zero debt is really about pursuing freedom,

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:18.991
a milestone of taking complete control of my own life.

00:09:19.292 --> 00:09:21.794
Now, viewers who know me well know that my family background actually

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:22.662
wasn't all that great.

00:09:22.828 --> 00:09:25.498
When I was a kid, I saw my parents worrying and borrowing money left and right

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:27.233
for our education,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.802
but these things didn't really traumatize me,

00:09:29.869 --> 00:09:31.804
nor did they make me hate debt.

00:09:32.438 --> 00:09:34.941
Maybe it's because it didn't happen to me directly,

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:36.809
and my parents got out of debt pretty quickly.

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:37.777
Actually, like a lot of people,

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:39.445
I kind of liked debt too.

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:41.080
For example, after I started working in Canada,

00:09:41.147 --> 00:09:43.516
I started leasing cars, one after another,

00:09:43.582 --> 00:09:46.319
and at one point I was making money and started getting a bit cocky,

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.187
going through all kinds of lifestyle creep,

00:09:48.254 --> 00:09:50.156
and I also took out a mortgage to buy a house.

00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:52.858
I remember my dad being super worried about us getting a mortgage

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:53.259
back then,

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:56.262
he heard it would take 25 years to pay off and just kept shaking his head.

00:09:56.729 --> 00:09:58.464
And at the time, I even laughed at him for being a stubborn old man,

00:09:58.531 --> 00:09:59.432
not knowing how to use leverage.

00:09:59.665 --> 00:10:02.268
So I'm not the type to absolutely despise debt,

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:04.637
and honestly, these debts didn't put too much

00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:05.204
financial pressure on me,

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:06.539
let alone any sort of trauma.

00:10:07.006 --> 00:10:09.508
The thing that really changed my perspective on debt,

00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:10.776
and made me start pursuing zero debt,

00:10:10.843 --> 00:10:14.180
was discovering the FIRE movement back in 2016,

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:16.849
and 2016 also happened to be when I bought this house.

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.853
I was reading this book, "Early Retirement

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:21.520
Extreme,"

00:10:21.587 --> 00:10:23.990
which introduced me to the concept of FIRE,

00:10:24.056 --> 00:10:26.559
and completely changed how I view the world and the trajectory of my

00:10:26.625 --> 00:10:27.059
life.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:30.496
And my pursuit of zero debt was also inspired by it.

00:10:30.663 --> 00:10:32.498
I've actually talked about this book on the channel before,

00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:34.200
that video has almost 800,000 views now,

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:35.468
and it's impacted a lot of people.

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:38.437
Later, I read a ton of books on financial independence, index investing,

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:40.406
and value investing, kicking off my journey to financial freedom.

00:10:40.873 --> 00:10:41.941
And from these books,

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:44.844
as well as from many veteran investors, the most profound

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.946
lesson I learned was: don't have debt.

00:10:47.913 --> 00:10:50.249
So in 2019, we bought a car entirely in cash for the first time,

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:51.217
getting rid of car loans,

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:52.852
and we're still driving that same car today.

00:10:53.252 --> 00:10:55.421
Later, our investments performed way beyond our expectations,

00:10:55.488 --> 00:10:57.823
helping us achieve a relative level of financial freedom early.

00:10:58.324 --> 00:11:00.393
We've never used leverage in our investments,

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:04.697
and our only remaining debt, our mortgage, became financially

00:11:04.764 --> 00:11:05.564
insignificant.

00:11:05.798 --> 00:11:09.535
Two years ago, I suddenly had a heart attack. We were terrified,

00:11:09.602 --> 00:11:12.438
but not for a single second was I worried about our future financial

00:11:12.505 --> 00:11:12.872
situation.

00:11:12.938 --> 00:11:13.305
In that moment,

00:11:13.372 --> 00:11:15.975
I was so grateful that I had read those books ten years ago,

00:11:16.042 --> 00:11:18.711
giving me this obsession with FIRE and financial freedom,

00:11:18.778 --> 00:11:21.580
making me focus heavily on my margin of safety,

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:24.817
ensuring that we never got thrown off during the roller coasters of investing

00:11:24.884 --> 00:11:25.851
and of life.

00:11:26.085 --> 00:11:27.219
Now, ten years have passed,

00:11:27.286 --> 00:11:29.789
and I actually don't care that much about FIRE and financial freedom anymore,

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.123
and I have some new perspectives on them,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.491
which I'll share with you guys later.

00:11:32.925 --> 00:11:36.195
But no matter how nuanced and detailed my understanding of debt has become,

00:11:36.262 --> 00:11:38.597
I still can't shake this one thought:

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:43.569
that I want to be completely debt-free, and as soon as I'm able to,

00:11:43.636 --> 00:11:44.070
I absolutely have to make it happen.

00:11:44.136 --> 00:11:45.705
And today was really, really interesting,

00:11:45.805 --> 00:11:49.275
because the bank manager who helped me pay off the mortgage remembered

00:11:49.341 --> 00:11:49.708
me,

00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:51.477
because while we were paying it off,

00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:52.645
he asked, "How did you get this money?"

00:11:52.711 --> 00:11:53.779
"How do you do your investing?"

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.314
And then suddenly he goes,

00:11:55.381 --> 00:11:56.749
"Are you the Tesla guy?"

00:11:57.516 --> 00:11:59.018
And I was like, "What? How do you know me?"

00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:03.656
He said, "Five years ago, you sold some Tesla stock to buy an

00:12:03.722 --> 00:12:04.724
investment property,"

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.559
"and when you brought a check in,"

00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:08.761
"I was the one who helped you process it."

00:12:08.828 --> 00:12:10.496
"And I've always remembered that,"

00:12:10.563 --> 00:12:13.466
"and today you're coming in to pay off your own mortgage."

00:12:13.632 --> 00:12:15.968
Wow, this was just such a crazy coincidence, so crazy.

00:12:16.635 --> 00:12:19.138
And then, after hearing that, we just cracked up laughing,

00:12:19.205 --> 00:12:21.707
and we even took a photo together to

00:12:21.774 --> 00:12:22.208
celebrate.

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:23.409
It's like we both witnessed

00:12:23.476 --> 00:12:24.910
one person's journey of investing and personal growth

00:12:25.044 --> 00:12:29.048
over the last five to ten years.

00:12:29.115 --> 00:12:33.219
So if the vision of being debt-free ten years ago marked

00:12:33.285 --> 00:12:35.888
the beginning of my personal investment journey and transformation,

00:12:35.955 --> 00:12:38.157
then actually achieving it ten years later

00:12:38.224 --> 00:12:41.193
marks the completion of a major milestone in my life.

00:12:41.694 --> 00:12:44.864
It's not just checking a box on a past goal,

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:46.999
it also represents the start of my new vision for life,

00:12:47.066 --> 00:12:50.002
which is the pursuit of a simple life.

00:12:50.069 --> 00:12:54.540
Let's think about that seesaw again, just imagine,

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:56.008
what if you didn't have that system?

00:12:56.075 --> 00:12:58.944
What if you didn't need to maintain that leverage at all?

00:12:59.011 --> 00:13:00.479
Then what do you have?

00:13:00.546 --> 00:13:03.616
Solid ground. Your life, your investments,

00:13:03.682 --> 00:13:05.684
your freedom, your choices are all built on it.

00:13:06.152 --> 00:13:08.387
It's simple, it's solid, it's beautiful,

00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:09.255
and it's anti-fragile.

00:13:09.855 --> 00:13:11.423
I want this simple life,

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:14.026
and when I have the ability to get it by being debt-free,

00:13:14.093 --> 00:13:16.162
I would regret it in the future if I didn't try.

00:13:16.695 --> 00:13:19.498
So being debt-free is reasonable for me,

00:13:19.565 --> 00:13:22.067
and I believe it's reasonable for a lot of people too.

00:13:22.134 --> 00:13:26.205
After all, the original intention of investing for many of us is just for

00:13:26.272 --> 00:13:26.839
freedom.

00:13:26.906 --> 00:13:29.308
Alright, so if you also want to pay off your mortgage early

00:13:29.375 --> 00:13:30.109
and pursue zero debt,

00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:33.045
I'll quickly share with you guys the specific methods I used to pay off my

00:13:33.112 --> 00:13:34.046
mortgage,

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.281
and give you some advice.

00:13:35.815 --> 00:13:38.184
Now, the specific operations for bank loans vary

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:38.817
from country to country.

00:13:38.884 --> 00:13:42.388
The following are things I started doing immediately after

00:13:42.454 --> 00:13:43.255
I got my mortgage.

00:13:43.689 --> 00:13:45.758
Number one, I increased my payment frequency,

00:13:45.825 --> 00:13:48.294
going from paying once a month to paying every two weeks.

00:13:48.661 --> 00:13:50.696
Number two, I increased my payment amount.

00:13:50.763 --> 00:13:52.998
I remember my bank allowed me to increase my original amount

00:13:53.065 --> 00:13:53.666
by 10 percent,

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:56.168
I forget the exact number, I just remember maxing it

00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:56.402
out.

00:13:56.468 --> 00:13:56.702
Number three,

00:13:56.769 --> 00:14:00.306
my bank also allowed me to make an annual lump-sum payment of

00:14:00.372 --> 00:14:01.473
10 percent of the loan amount.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.776
I didn't use this a ton, I only did it three times.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.512
Number four, my loan was a closed mortgage,

00:14:06.579 --> 00:14:09.481
meaning if I wanted to pay it all off early, there'd be a penalty,

00:14:09.548 --> 00:14:12.251
and I could only pay it off completely without penalty when renewing every five

00:14:12.318 --> 00:14:12.585
years.

00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:14.320
So this was the chance to renew,

00:14:14.386 --> 00:14:15.387
so I just paid it off.

00:14:15.955 --> 00:14:17.790
Of course, these aren't the only ways.

00:14:17.856 --> 00:14:20.459
You need to make a plan based on

00:14:20.526 --> 00:14:20.893
your actual mortgage situation.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.230
But honestly, the methods themselves aren't that important.

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:25.698
What's more important is one key principle:

00:14:25.764 --> 00:14:30.269
don't let paying off debt put you back

00:14:30.336 --> 00:14:31.270
in a dangerous spot.

00:14:31.904 --> 00:14:34.373
Remember at the start of the video when I said I paid off the

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:34.540
mortgage

00:14:34.607 --> 00:14:36.208
because I *could*?

00:14:36.375 --> 00:14:38.510
That's exactly what I meant by "could."

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:42.047
It means you still need to have savings, an emergency fund,

00:14:42.114 --> 00:14:43.983
a margin of safety, and necessary insurance.

00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:47.086
You can't let paying off debt push your life into a corner.

00:14:47.586 --> 00:14:48.387
In other words,

00:14:48.454 --> 00:14:50.990
the prerequisite for paying off debt is keeping a positive cash flow.

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:53.425
Your income, minus debts and expenses,

00:14:53.492 --> 00:14:54.727
and investments, should still leave you with a surplus.

00:14:55.094 --> 00:14:58.430
For example, five years ago, mathematically, I could have paid off the

00:14:58.497 --> 00:14:58.964
mortgage.

00:14:59.031 --> 00:15:01.934
And I wanted to. At the time, without even selling stocks,

00:15:02.001 --> 00:15:03.836
scraping together the cash was totally doable.

00:15:03.903 --> 00:15:06.405
But after paying it off, I'd have zero cash left,

00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:07.473
which was unacceptable to me.

00:15:07.673 --> 00:15:09.808
Plus, we were in the middle of the pandemic. Everything was uncertain.

00:15:09.875 --> 00:15:10.676
Cash was king.

00:15:10.776 --> 00:15:13.479
So even though I really wanted to, I couldn't.

00:15:13.979 --> 00:15:15.948
But three years ago, we finally could.

00:15:16.015 --> 00:15:18.684
Our cash reserves minus the entire mortgage were enough to live on for a

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:19.184
good few years.

00:15:19.251 --> 00:15:22.321
So we just waited for the next renewal to pay it off in one

00:15:22.388 --> 00:15:23.122
lump sum.

00:15:23.188 --> 00:15:24.923
So when it comes to paying off a mortgage,

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.859
I've always done what I could,

00:15:26.926 --> 00:15:30.696
but I never let it become a major psychological or financial

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:31.530
burden.

00:15:31.597 --> 00:15:33.599
We always say you shouldn't go all in on investing,

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:36.635
because you'll definitely expose yourself to unexpected

00:15:36.702 --> 00:15:37.369
risks.

00:15:37.436 --> 00:15:41.407
Well, paying off debt is essentially an investment too, and you can't go all

00:15:41.473 --> 00:15:42.274
in.

00:15:42.374 --> 00:15:44.877
Actually, for most people, a mortgage might be the only time in your life

00:15:44.943 --> 00:15:48.814
you can get such huge leverage relatively easily.

00:15:48.881 --> 00:15:49.848
You should make good use of it.

00:15:50.516 --> 00:15:53.952
And the bottom line for using it well is not letting it destroy you.

00:15:54.586 --> 00:15:56.522
Excessive debt can destroy you,

00:15:56.588 --> 00:15:59.391
but excessive debt repayment can destroy you too.

00:16:00.025 --> 00:16:01.860
So my advice is,

00:16:01.927 --> 00:16:05.364
only reduce your debt when you are truly able to

00:16:05.431 --> 00:16:05.998
do so.

00:16:06.065 --> 00:16:09.001
But at the same time, don't forget—if you want to be debt-free,

00:16:09.068 --> 00:16:11.937
when you actually *can*, remember to do it.

00:16:12.004 --> 00:16:13.038
Don't forget your original intention.

00:16:13.105 --> 00:16:15.474
OK, thanks for watching this video.

00:16:15.541 --> 00:16:16.709
Before making this video,

00:16:16.775 --> 00:16:19.745
I started a discussion thread in the Yikouxinfan community,

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.448
got a ton of replies, and it gave me a lot of inspiration.

00:16:22.548 --> 00:16:24.616
So huge thanks to our community members.

00:16:24.683 --> 00:16:27.419
And you watching this, you're welcome to join our community too,

00:16:27.486 --> 00:16:29.288
where we all learn, share, and grow together.

00:16:29.588 --> 00:16:31.457
Thanks for the support, see you next time, bye-bye.

00:16:33.625 --> 00:16:34.426
[Outro Music]

